// REJECTS: An exception of type 'NullPointerException' occurred
//
// UPSTREAM DEFECT, pinned deliberately.
//
// `serviceOutputValue: (identifier | dslNavigationPath)` is ONE rule with two meanings. The
// walker chooses which half is real by the enclosing dataset kind, not by what was written:
//
//   visitTdsDatasetKeys        ctx.serviceOutputValue().identifier()
//   visitGraphFetchDatasetKeys ctx.serviceOutputValue().dslNavigationPath()
//
// (PersistenceParseTreeWalker.java:416-433.) Neither checks. Write a navigation path under a
// TDS service output and `.identifier()` returns null, which is then passed straight to
// PureGrammarParserUtility.fromIdentifier -- so legend-engine answers a type error in the
// user's input with "please notify developer" and no source information.
//
// The fixture is here because the input MUST be rejected and a conforming parser has to
// reject it; the fact that the rejection arrives as an NPE is the defect, and if upstream
// ever fixes it into a real parser error this fixture fails and says so.
###Persistence
Persistence test::persistence::neg::TdsKeysAsPaths
{
  doc: 'a TDS service output whose keys are graph-fetch navigation paths';
  trigger: Manual;
  service: test::persistence::Service;
  serviceOutputTargets:
  [
    TDS
    {
      keys:
      [
        #/test::example::MyType/prop#
      ]
      datasetType: Delta
      {
        actionIndicator: None;
      }
    }
    ->
    {
    }
  ];
}
